I consider the protection racket to be a false economy,
based on the assumption that gold is scarce -- which
is true in the real world but not in nethack.

In the very early game, survival is very difficult, and killing
things and gaining levels improves your hitpoints and
strength and trains your weapon skills, all of which makes
survival *much* easier.  Buying protection lowers your
AC, which also helps, but the *first* time you buy
protection it gives you 2-4 points and subsequently
you only get 1, so you get the biggest bang for your buck
the first time.  If you have, say, 2200 zm by the time you
reach Minetown, at XL1 you can get 5-7 points of AC
(with 200 zm left for shopping); whereas, at XL4 you can
still get 2-4 (again, with 200zm left).  Bear in mind, gold
becomes more and more common as you go, so you can
keep popping back in to Minetown to buy *more* protection
after every couple of levels, until eventually the priests
stop rewarding your devotion.  Typically by the time you
get to the castle or so (perhaps a little later if the RNG
was a little stingier) you will have money sitting around
in stashes that's too heavy to carry all the time and no
really compelling use for it: you've bought the entire
inventory of every shop in the dungeon (except maybe
the junk armor and such) and as much protection as
the priests will sell you.

So you're balancing the value of having your AC be
maybe 3 points lower _after_ Minetown (assuming you
can survive to get to Minetown -- the first two levels in
the mines can be quite deadly for an XL1 character)
versus the extra hitpoints you get from gaining about
three experience levels (which starts accruing even
_before_ you get to Minetown).

Either way, later in the game you're going to have
both, so the only question is which option makes the
early game easier to survive.  I think I know the answer.


